		},
		{
			"id":"ddb70a2b5eb93bcf",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"# Electric Susceptibility\n\nThe electric susceptibility is a complex quantity in general usually denoted as\n$$\\chi(\\omega)=\\chi'(\\omega)+i\\chi''(\\omega)$$\nbased on the Lorentz model for bound electrons in a solid the real and imaginary parts look as follows\n$$\\chi(\\omega)=\\frac{{\\omega^{2} _{p}}}{(w^{2}_{res}-\\omega^2)^2+(\\gamma \\omega)^2}\\Big[(w^{2}_{res}-\\omega^2)+i\\gamma \\omega \\Big]$$\n![[../attachments/Pasted image 20230425095500.png|center|600]]\n\nThe Lorentzian in blue is $\\chi''$ and the line in red is $\\chi'$",
			"type":"text",
			"x":900,
			"y":580,
			"width":740,
			"height":800
		},
		{
			"id":"5b306e1369f92124",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"# Nomenclature\n\nA **nondispersive** medium responds instantaneously to electric field (like vacuum).\n\nA **homogenous** medium has position independent dielectric constant\n\nAn **isotropic** medium has a dielectric constant that is independent of orientation (it is a scalar quantity as opposed to tensor quantity)\n\n",
			"type":"text",
			"x":800,
			"y":-460,
			"width":440,
			"height":440
		},
		{
			"id":"3f0dfcf778d85734",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"# Plasma Frequency\n\n$$\\omega_{p} = \\sqrt{ \\frac{Nq^2}{m^{*}_{q}\\epsilon_{0}}  }$$",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-360,
			"y":535,
			"width":396,
			"height":173
		},
		{
			"id":"0ef6f816ca631d01",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"# Drude Lorentz Model\nWe treat the electrons bound to the nucleus as if they were damped harmonic oscillators. The equation of motion for the deviation from equilibrium is given as\n$$\\mathbf{\\ddot{u}}+\\gamma \\mathbf{\\dot{u}}+\\omega_{res}^{2}  \\mathbf{u}=-\\frac{e}{m_{e}}\\mathbf{E}$$\n==since $\\gamma$ is proportional to collision rate, and $\\omega_{res}$ is the resonant frequency in the optical range, it is reasonable to assume that $\\gamma\\ll \\omega_{res}$ since the collision rate is much slower==\nThe solution is given by\n$$\\mathbf{u}(\\omega)= -\\frac{e / m^{*}_{e}}{(\\omega_{res}^2-\\omega^2 -i\\gamma \\omega)}\\mathbf{E}(\\omega)$$\nwhich for monochromatic light $\\hat{E}\\propto \\delta(\\omega-\\omega_{0})$ is\n$$\\mathbf{u}(t)= -\\frac{e / m^{*}_{e}}{(\\omega_{res}^2-\\omega^2 _{0} -i\\gamma \\omega_{0})}\\mathbf{E}_{0}e^{ -i\\omega_{0}t }$$\nThe electric dipole density is given by\n$$\\mathbf{\\hat{P}}=\\sum\\limits_{i}\\hat{\\mathbf{p}_{i}}=-Ne\\mathbf{\\hat{u}}=\\epsilon_{0}\\chi \\mathbf{\\hat{E}}$$\nwhich in turn gives us the susceptibility\n$$\\chi(\\omega) = \\frac{\\omega_{p}^{2}}{\\omega_{res}^{2}-\\omega^{2}-i \\gamma \\omega}, \\quad \\quad \\omega_{p}^{2} = \\frac{Ne^{2}}{m_{e}^{*}\\epsilon_{0}}$$\n\n",
			"type":"text",
			"x":128,
			"y":250,
			"width":555,
			"height":920
		},
		{
			"id":"6908db6ab23321ce",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"# Electromagnetic Response\nelectric $(\\mathbf{E})$ and magnetic $(\\mathbf{H})$ fields induce electric polarization $(\\mathbf{P})$ and magnetization $(\\mathbf{M})$ inside materials\n$$\\begin{align}\n\\mathbf{D}&=\\epsilon_{0}\\mathbf{E}+\\mathbf{P}&\\approx \\epsilon_{0}(1+\\chi)\\mathbf{E}\\equiv \\epsilon \\mathbf{E} \\\\\n\\mathbf{B}&=\\mu_{0}\\mathbf{H}+\\mu_{0}\\mathbf{M} &\\approx \\mu_{0}(1+\\chi_{m})\\mathbf{H}\\equiv \\mu \\mathbf{H}\n\\end{align}$$\nWhile the approximation above holds for weak perturbations where the linear response is prominent. Most generally, there can be electric response induced by a magnetic field and vice versa given by\n$$\\begin{align}\n\\mathbf{D} &= \\epsilon \\mathbf{E} + \\xi \\mathbf{ H} \\\\\n\\mathbf{B} &= \\mu \\mathbf{H} +\\zeta \\mathbf{E} \n\\end{align}$$\nThe so called *constitutive relations* in which the cross relations are usually neglected due to their value being OOM smaller.",
			"type":"text",
			"x":86,
			"y":-460,
			"width":640,
			"height":540,
			"color":"5"
		}
